[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m To determine the greatest integer \( n \) such that \( n^2 - 11n + 24 \leq 0 \), we first need to find the roots of the equation \( n^2 - 11n + 24 = 0 \). We can factorize the quadratic expression as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m \[ n^2 - 11n + 24 = (n-3)(n-8). \]
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m So, the roots of the quadratic equation are \( n = 3 \) and \( n = 8 \). These roots divide the number line into three intervals: \( (-\infty, 3] \), \( [3, 8] \), and \( [8, \infty) \). We need to test the sign of the expression \( (n-3)(n-8) \) in each of these intervals.
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 1. For \( n \in (-\infty, 3] \), choose \( n = 2 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m    \[ (2-3)(2-8) = (-1)(-6) = 6 > 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m    So, the expression is positive in this interval.
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 2. For \( n \in [3, 8] \), choose \( n = 4 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m    \[ (4-3)(4-8) = (1)(-4) = -4 < 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m    So, the expression is negative in this interval.
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 3. For \( n \in [8, \infty) \), choose \( n = 9 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m    \[ (9-3)(9-8) = (6)(1) = 6 > 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m    So, the expression is positive in this interval.
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m Since we are looking for the smallest integer \( n \) such that \( n^2 - 11n + 24 \leq 0 \), we compare the signed values at the roots \( n = 3 \) and \( n = 8 \). The expression is zero at \( n = 3 \) and positive at \( n = 4 \) and \( n = 8 \). Therefore, the smallest integer \( n \) for which the expression is non-positive is \( n = 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3407)[0m Thus, the greatest integer \( n \) such that \( n^2 - 11n + 24 \leq 0 \) is \(\boxed{3}\).<|im_end|>
